UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 2, Lesson 2: Interviewing someone about the past', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"An oral history interview gathers memories from a person who was there. Good interviewers ask permission, prepare open questions that start with how, why or what, listen carefully, record accurately and say thank you.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nRewrite these as open questions: \"Was it fun?\" and \"Did you have a television?\"\n\nFEEDBACK\nFor example: \"What did you enjoy most, and why?\" and \"How did your family spend evenings and what did you use for entertainment?\"\n\nWATCH FOR\nAsking only yes or no questions, or interrupting, produces very short answers that give a historian little evidence.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWith adult permission, interview a trusted adult about the past of a place. Write six open questions. Record four facts, one opinion and the date. Do not share private details publicly.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"An oral history interview gathers memories from a person who was there. Good interviewers ask permission, prepare open questions that start with how, why or what, listen carefully, record accurately and say thank you.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and respond","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-HIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: With adult permission, interview a trusted adult about the past of a place. Write six open questions. Record four facts, one opinion and the date. Do not share private details publicly. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: Weak question: \"Did you like school?\" (yes or no). Better: \"What did you do at lunch when you were at school?\" Example notes for a fictional interviewee Mr Lee: walked two kilometres to school; played marbles at lunch; the hall was used for dances.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Asking only yes or no questions, or interrupting, produces very short answers that give a historian little evidence. Correction: For example: \"What did you enjoy most, and why?\" and \"How did your family spend evenings and what did you use for entertainment?\"\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Asking only yes or no questions, or interrupting, produces very short answers that give a historian little evidence. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"An oral history interview gathers memories from a person who was there. Good interviewers ask permission, prepare open questions that start with how, why or what, listen carefully, record accurately and say thank you. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["An oral history interview gathers memories from a person who was there. Good interviewers ask permission, prepare open questions that start with how, why or what, listen carefully, record accurately and say thank you.","Asking only yes or no questions, or interrupting, produces very short answers that give a historian little evidence.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Copy the model places and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and model\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking.","Copy the model places and call that independent work.","Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Show the evidence\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Asking only yes or no questions, or interrupting, produces very short answers that give a historian little evidence.","Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source.","Skip respectful protocols because the first idea looked neat."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Test a misconception\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence.","Skip respectful protocols because the first idea looked neat.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Transfer to a new inquiry\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"For example: \"What did you enjoy most, and why?\" and \"How did your family spend evenings and what did you use for entertainment?\" Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["For example: \"What did you enjoy most, and why?\" and \"How did your family spend evenings and what did you use for entertainment?\"","Asking only yes or no questions, or interrupting, produces very short answers that give a historian little evidence.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Rewrite these as open questions: \"Was it fun?\" and \"Did you have a television?\"\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["The most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"List the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"For example: \"What did you enjoy most, and why?\" and \"How did your family spend evenings and what did you use for entertainment?\" Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["For example: \"What did you enjoy most, and why?\" and \"How did your family spend evenings and what did you use for entertainment?\"","Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Asking only yes or no questions, or interrupting, produces very short answers that give a historian little evidence.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with e\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["The new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Inquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a \", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant evidence with accurate reasoning and named sources or places.","question":"Inquire into or describe a fresh example that uses the central skill. Show evidence and name the sources or places that matter.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: Weak question: \"Did you like school?\" (yes or no). Better: \"What did you do at lunch when you were at school?\" Example notes for a fictional interviewee Mr Lee: walked two kilometres to school; played marbles at lunch; the hall was used for dances.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change the claim or evidence, not just make the notes longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus corrected claim and an evidence-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely misconception for this HSIE topic, correct it and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Asking only yes or no questions, or interrupting, produces very short answers that give a historian little evidence. Correction: For example: \"What did you enjoy most, and why?\" and \"How did your family spend evenings and what did you use for entertainment?\"","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w02-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise interview.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"History","success_criteria":["I can use interview with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify question and explain why it matters.","I can practise oral history independently on a new prompt.","I can notice permission needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"An oral history interview gathers memories from a person who was there. Good interviewers ask permission, prepare open questions that start with how, why or what, listen carefully, record accurately and say thank you. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["An oral history interview gathers memories from a person who was there. Good interviewers ask permission, prepare open questions that start with how, why or what, listen carefully, record accurately and say thank you.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nWeak question: \"Did you like school?\" (yes or no). Better: \"What did you do at lunch when you were at school?\" Example notes for a fictional interviewee Mr Lee: walked two kilometres to school; played marbles at lunch; the hall was used for dances.\n\nTRY FIRST\nRewrite these as open questions: \"Was it fun?\" and \"Did you have a television?\"\n\nFEEDBACK\nFor example: \"What did you enjoy most, and why?\" and \"How did your family spend evenings and what did you use for entertainment?\"","explain":"An oral history interview gathers memories from a person who was there. Good interviewers ask permission, prepare open questions that start with how, why or what, listen carefully, record accurately and say thank you.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Asking only yes or no questions, or interrupting, produces very short answers that give a historian little evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Closed and open interview questions.","caption":"Open questions start with what, how or why.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Closed</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Did you like school?</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Open</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">What did you do at lunch?</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Weak question: \"Did you like school?\" (yes or no). Better: \"What did you do at lunch when you were at school?\" Example n Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Weak question: \"Did you like school?\" (yes or no). Better: \"What did you do at lunch when you were at school?\" Example n Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nWeak question: \"Did you like school?\" (yes or no). Better: \"What did you do at lunch when you were at school?\" Example notes for a fictional interviewee Mr Lee: walked two kilometres to school; played marbles at lunch; the hall was used for dances. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Separating facts from opinions in interview notes.","caption":"Record date and who spoke.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Notes</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">walked 2 km; played marbles</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Opinion</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">&quot;The hall was my favourite&quot;</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAsking only yes or no questions, or interrupting, produces very short answers that give a historian little evidence. Here is a more productive model: Weak question: \"Did you like school?\" (yes or no). Better: \"What did you do at lunch when you were at school?\" Example notes for a fictional interviewee Mr Lee: walked two kilometres to school; played marbles at lunch; the hall was used for dances.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nFor example: \"What did you enjoy most, and why?\" and \"How did your family spend evenings and what did you use for entertainment?\"","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Asking only yes or no questions, or interrupting, produces very short answers that give a historian little evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAn oral history interview gathers memories from a person who was there. Good interviewers ask permission, prepare open questions that start with how, why or what, listen carefully, record accurately and say thank you. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Transfer to a new inquiry"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 2, Lesson 2: Interviewing someone about the past","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Our local community and its history","week":2,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nWeak question: \"Did you like school?\" (yes or no). Better: \"What did you do at lunch when you were at school?\" Example notes for a fictional interviewee Mr Lee: walked two kilometres to school; played marbles at lunch; the hall was used for dances.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the sources, places or community details used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Closed and open interview questions.","caption":"Open questions start with what, how or why.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Closed</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Did you like school?</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Open</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">What did you do at lunch?</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Separating facts from opinions in interview notes.","caption":"Record date and who spoke.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Notes</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">walked 2 km; played marbles</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Opinion</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">&quot;The hall was my favourite&quot;</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-hsie-y3-w02-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
